<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel González Flores</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel González Flores was a Mexican general and president from 1880 to 1884, a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>, who held the office between Díaz's first and second terms. His government extended railways and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines but faced charges of corruption and riots over a new nickel coinage. Díaz returned to the presidency in 1884.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-gonzalez-flores.jpg" alt="Manuel González Flores" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Manuel_Gonzalez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel González Flores was born at Matamoros, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, on 17 June 1833, though some sources give 20 June. He began his military career in 1847, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> invaders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a> after they killed his father. From 1853 to 1855 he fought with the Conservative forces and in 1859 took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-miramon">Miguel Miramón</a>'s attack on Veracruz against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a>'s government. In 1860 he took an amnesty and offered his services to the Liberals.</p><p>He fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a> against the French, was wounded and captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-puebla">Battle of Puebla</a> on 5 May 1862 but escaped, and lost his right arm at the siege of Puebla in 1867. He supported Díaz in the revolt of the Plan de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noria">Noria</a> and in the successful revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-tuxtepec">Plan of Tuxtepec</a> in 1876, and became general of division in 1877.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Díaz could not seek re-election in 1880, since his coup against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-lerdo-de-tejada">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada</a> rested on the principle of no re-election, and he promoted González, who had been his secretary of war from 1878 to 1879. González was announced as winner on 24 September 1880 with 11,528 votes and served from 1 December 1880 to 30 November 1884. He continued Díaz's policy of order and progress and of foreign investment. A mining code in 1884 gave landowners rights to the subsoil, and a land law let the government sell vacant lands. He raised military spending by 400 percent, built a loyal rural police, and resolved a dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and Soconusco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s favour.</p><p>Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were renewed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> recognised a British bond debt of 11.5 million pounds, which brought protests in Congress and riots in the capital that were put down forcefully. The Banco Nacional de México was founded, the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> to Paso del Norte was built, and Mexico's first submarine cable opened. Nickel coins issued in 1882 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and riots on 21 December 1883.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Díaz succeeded him in 1884. Congress charged González with misappropriating public funds in 1885, but the charges were dropped on 30 October 1888. He was elected governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> unanimously in 1884, served three terms, and died of pancreatic cancer on 8 May 1893. The presidency has been judged corrupt, but that view is coloured by the financial difficulties of 1884 and by Díaz's campaign to discredit his successor, according to the account of his presidency. His biographer Don M. Coerver wrote the one full scholarly study of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel González Flores” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Heroica Matamoros, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Chapingo, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.85,-97.5&amp;year=1872" aria-label="Heroica Matamoros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel González Flores"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4424 5336h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4424 5336h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.85,-97.5&amp;year=1872">What was here in 1872? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
